# known-failures.allow — the PostgreSQL regression-suite ratchet ledger.
# Companion: scripts/rowsort_compare.py --allow-file. Precedent:
# crates/_support/seams_init/tests/lint-determinism.allow.
#
# One test name per line. A listed test may fail without failing CI; a test
# NOT listed that fails breaks the build, and a listed test that stops failing
# is reported stale and also breaks the build, so this file can only shrink.
# Removing an entry is the unit of progress.
#
# RESEEDED: 2026-09-05 (second time that day) against main @ ead804de2f, built
# with the PGDG oracle pinned to 18.3. 230 tests checked, 218 exact, 12 fail,
# 0 error; isolation 131/131. Reproduced independently on a GitHub runner
# (run 33965983742) and locally.
#
# WHY IT SHRANK 20 -> 12, AND THE MISTAKE THAT PUT IT AT 20. The earlier
# reseed that day was measured against an image still carrying PGDG 18.6,
# because the ledger was rewritten BEFORE the oracle was pinned to 18.3 and
# the suite was never re-run afterwards. Eight entries were therefore harness
# artefacts, not defects:
#
#     alter_table  copy  copy2  copyselect
#     generated_stored  generated_virtual  privileges  rowsecurity
#
# The pgrust binary is byte-identical between the two images
# (sha256 030758105b70...), so nothing about pgrust changed; only the C
# tooling did. For copyselect the mechanism is visible in the diff: under
# psql 18.6 a failing `COPY (SELECT ...) FROM STDIN` leaves the inline data
# block unconsumed, psql emits "invalid command \.", then feeds the data
# lines back as SQL and the rest of the file desynchronises -- 63 lines
# produced against 161 expected. Under psql 18.3, the release pgrust targets,
# the same server handles it correctly. The COPY entries and the two
# generated-column entries share that "output stops early" signature;
# alter_table, privileges and rowsecurity were not individually characterised
# before they started passing.
#
# That divergence is worth keeping rather than discarding: it is a
# forward-compatibility signal against PostgreSQL 18.6, and belongs to
# UPSTREAM.md's 18.4/18.6 triage.
#
# The remaining 12 are ONE class. Every one is plan shape: pgrust chooses a
# different plan (frequently a parallel one -- Gather / Workers Planned: 1 /
# Parallel Seq Scan -- where C's expected output is serial) and every changed
# line sits inside an EXPLAIN block. No result row differs in any of them.
# EXPLAIN output is not part of the SQL contract, so closing these means
# deciding, per test, whether the shape difference is permitted; no
# adjudication of this territory exists on main yet and one has to be written.
#
# NOT an entry: numeric_big. It is overlaid but absent from parallel_schedule
# (upstream runs it only via EXTRA_TESTS), so it never executes and the
# comparator reports it as unchecked rather than scoring it.

create_index
incremental_sort
join
limit
memoize
partition_aggregate
partition_join
partition_prune
portals
select_distinct
subselect
tidscan
